Yes, absolutely. Every single programming language has specific versions that are known to be vulnerable to security flaws. [1, 2] 
No software is perfect. When core developers write a programming language, they make mistakes just like anyone else. When those mistakes create security holes, they are documented as CVEs (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) against that specific version of the language runtime or interpreter. [3, 4, 5, 6] 
## Famous Examples of Vulnerable Language Versions

* Python (Versions below 3.11.4): Older versions suffered from a flaw where processing certain web links could trigger an internal memory crash, allowing hackers to freeze the server (Denial of Service). [7, 8, 9] 
* PHP (Version 8.1.0 to 8.1.28): A critical bug discovered in 2024 allowed attackers to bypass security protections completely on Windows servers and run malicious commands remotely (CVE-2024-4577). [10, 11, 12, 13] 
* Java / JDK (Older updates of Java 8, 11, 17): A famous bug nicknamed "Psychic Signatures" (CVE-2022-21449) allowed hackers to easily forge digital SSL certificates and access secure systems because of a flaw in Java's math logic. [14, 15, 16] 

## How Language Vulnerabilities Act Differently Than Library Bugs
If a library has a bug, you can usually just swap it out or update that single package.
If the language version itself has a bug, the entire foundation is broken. Every single application, script, and library running on that machine becomes exposed because the underlying engine (the Python interpreter, the Java Virtual Machine, etc.) is unsafe. [17, 18] 
## How to Stay Safe
Security teams use two main strategies to handle this:

   1. End-of-Life (EOL) Tracking: They stop using language versions that are no longer supported. For example, Python 2 and Python 3.7 are completely retired; they receive zero security updates, making them permanently unsafe. [19, 20, 21, 22] 
   2. Patch Management: They use automated tools to ensure the server's runtime environment updates immediately when a language vendor releases a security patch. [23] 

